    "evidence_text": "Bax translocation and mitochondrial fragmentation induced by Helicobacter pylori.\n\nOmi/HtrA2 deficiency caused an accumulation of reactive oxygen species and reduced mitochondrial membrane potential.\n\nElectron microscopy confirmed the mitochondrial morphology alterations and showed abnormal cristae structure.\n\nOur results underscore a critical role of impaired mitochondrial dynamics in neurodegenerative disorders.\n\nBioenergetic aspects of apoptosis, necrosis and mitoptosis\n\nThe following conclusions are made.\n\nMassive mitoptosis can result in cell death due to release to cytosol of the cell death proteins normally hidden in the mitochondrial intermembrane space.\n\nHere we show that both neurons and cancer cells strictly inhibit cytochrome c-mediated apoptosis by a mechanism dependent on glucose metabolism.\n\nBAX/BAK-independent mitoptosis during cell death induced by proteasome inhibition?\n\nProteasome inhibitors induce rapid death of cancer cells.\n\nSubsequent cell death is independent of BAX, and probably BAK, and proceeds through the intrinsic mitochondrial apoptosis pathway.\n\nThese data indicate that c-myc expression is a necessary component of activation-induced apoptosis.\n\nRecent studies suggest a close relationship between cell metabolism and apoptosis.\n\nAt the gates of death.\n\nThis essential step is controlled and mediated by proteins of the Bcl-2 family.",
